{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794011261,"nonce":"DdugMH0bpfd1bKqpHOthBuHggf4=","message":"Card border artifact on the WebGL2 path, 2026-11-07. cn/card live demo on the Pixel 10 Pro (worker-webgl2) draws a beaded, dotted bright line along the inner edge of the 1px border where it follows the corner radius, faintly along the straight top edge too, and the border is about half as bright as on WebGPU (peak +12 over the card fill against +22, same 3px width, at dpr 3). Reproduced on a Mac with ANGLE/Metal by rewriting the worker script to hide navigator.gpu (live tab then reports worker-webgl2): same beads, peak 43 against 53. So it is the GL pipeline (vb vertex fetch + dt fragment shaders, non-sRGB Rgba8Unorm surface), not PowerVR. The border code in sdf_core_dt.wgsl matches sdf_core.wgsl line for line, so suspects are the blend/colour-space difference (gamma-space blending of the semi-transparent border, which would also explain the dimmer stroke) and the seam where the fill's outer edge and the border's inner edge each antialias separately. Next: reproduce on the desktop GL path with a minimal bordered rounded rect and compare coverage maths.","files":null}]}